
# a few outputs from claude:


Possible Improvements
Here are 5 concrete improvements, ranging from bug fixes to features:
1. Fix memory leaks in state transitions (bug)
In timer_running.cpp and timer_running_break.cpp, new Menu/MenuItem[] objects are allocated with new each time a confirmation state is entered, but the previous pointer is overwritten without delete[]. On an embedded system with limited RAM, this will eventually cause crashes or instability after enough work/break cycles.
2. Replace magic preset-name string comparisons with an enum
icon_provider.h and timer_running.cpp use strcmp("Coding"), strcmp("Emails"), etc. to select icons and behavior. These are fragile — rename a preset in one place and the icons silently break. Replacing this with a typed PresetType enum would make the code safe and self-documenting.
3. Batch NVS reads/writes in preferences_manager
Currently preferences.begin() and preferences.end() are called on every single read or write. Each begin() involves an NVS lock and flash scan. For operations like loading all settings at startup or saving statistics at end-of-session, a session-level open/close would be significantly faster and reduce flash wear.
4. Add a custom preset editor via the settings menu
Currently the three presets (Coding, Emails, Meetings) are hardcoded in main.cpp with fixed work/break durations. Adding a settings screen that lets the user adjust durations (or even names) via the rotary encoder — persisted to NVS — would make the device genuinely configurable without reflashing.
5. Per-preset statistics and a history screen
statistics.h tracks only global totals (total cycles, total work time, total break time). Tracking per-preset stats and adding a dedicated "Stats" screen accessible from the main menu would give the user meaningful productivity insights — e.g., how many coding sessions vs. email sessions per day.


** lump them in groups: mem leaks + batch NVS reads/writes as an efficiency PR. Stats can come early on its own. 



